package dev.cajeta.llm;

import cajeta.lang.String;

/**
 * Engine diagnostics — OFF by default, and off means nothing is written
 * to any stream.
 *
 * These lines exist for a good reason (a silent fallback that produces
 * CORRECT output is indistinguishable from a failed optimization — the
 * batched-prefill route once read as "batching bought 4%" when it had
 * never engaged), but they are the engine talking to whoever is tuning
 * it, not to the user of whatever is embedding the engine.
 *
 * They were briefly written to stderr, which is worse than stdout rather
 * than better: stderr is a SEVERITY channel, so IDEs and log collectors
 * paint it red and alert on it, and "we picked the batched kernel" gets
 * laundered into "something went wrong". A combined console just sees
 * noise. Julian called this out 2026-08-27.
 *
 * So: a hook, not a dependency. The engine is a LIBRARY — it must not
 * pick a logging backend for its embedder, and cajeta has no env-var API
 * to read a level from, so enabling is an explicit call. An application
 * installs a sink — cabra AND the `cajeta-llm` CLI both bridge to
 * `dev.cajeta.logging` at DEBUG, through `Log.at` so the lines aggregate
 * into the host's single stream rather than opening a second one — and
 * with no sink installed `say` is a couple of predictable branches.
 *
 * The CLI briefly used a plain stderr sink (2026-08-30) and that was
 * wrong for the reason stated above; it is gone, and nothing in this
 * project writes diagnostics to stderr any more. That makes stderr
 * meaningful again: output there is a fault, not chatter.
 *
 * Call sites MUST guard with `Diag.on()`. `sayRoute` runs per projection
 * and the strings concatenate several fields; building them only to drop
 * them on the floor is a cost every embedder would pay for a feature
 * almost none of them turn on.
 */
public final class Diag {

    // null == silent, and that is the default.
    //
    // Writing this the obvious way used to CRASH the compiler: comparing a
    // static field of interface type against null hit
    // "ICmpInst::AssertOK(): Both operands to ICmp instruction are not of
    // the same type!", and the same path SIGSEGV'd at runtime for a plain
    // `Sink s = null` local. Both are fixed in cajeta as of 2026-08-27
    // (InterfaceStaticNullCompareTests), so this reads naturally again
    // instead of carrying a parallel boolean.
    static DiagSink sink;

    public Diag() { return; }

    /** Install a sink. Passing null turns diagnostics back off. */
    public static void setSink(DiagSink s) {
        Diag.sink = s;
        return;
    }

    /** Whether anything is listening. Guard every call site with this. */
    public static boolean on() {
        return Diag.sink != null;
    }

    /** Emit one diagnostic line, if anything is listening. */
    public static void say(String message) {
        if (Diag.sink != null) {
            Diag.sink.line(message);
        }
        return;
    }
}
